{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '215,245p' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py; sed -n '1,210p' task/task.md; cat results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_ilr_residual_geometry/program_1/src/IMPLEMENTATION.md; cat results/ideas/03_continuous_boundary_covariance_interpolation/program_1/src/IMPLEMENTATION.md; rg -n 'C[1-6]|dirichlet|logistic|shift|alpha|strat|uniform|seed|n_train|n_cal|n_test|def _|case' task/evaluate.py | head -100; sed -n '1,210p' results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_ilr_residual_geometry/program_1/src/program.py\"","aggregated_output":"\n\ndef parse_args() -> argparse.Namespace:\n    \"\"\"Parse command-line arguments.\"\"\"\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Append one memory event, or atomically rewrite conclusions.md.\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--write-conclusions\",\n        action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Switch into conclusions-write mode (validates and replaces conclusions.md).\",\n    )\n    # Event-append mode (existing behavior).\n    parser.add_argument(\"--event-json\", default=None, help=\"Event payload as JSON object string\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--event-file\", default=None, help=\"Path to event JSON file\")\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--local-memory-md\",\n        default=\"./memory.md\",\n        help=\"Local workspace memory markdown path\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--global-memory-md\",\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Global markdown memory path (event mode)\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--global-memory-jsonl\",\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Global JSONL memory path (event mode)\",\n    )\n    # Conclusions-write mode.\n# Goal\n\n**研究任务：组成型（和为 1 的向量）预测输出的保形不确定性评价**\n**问题与动机**\n许多预测器的输出是一个“组成”：非负且和为 1 的向量，例如分类器的 softmax 概率、主题比例等。使用者不只关心点预测是否准确，也需要判断预测附带的不确定性估计是否可靠。\n\n保形预测（conformal prediction）在可交换性假设下为预测集提供有限样本的覆盖率保证，并有多种实现和校准方式。这些方法在组成型输出上如何比较和评价，尚缺乏统一、可复现的做法。\n\n**核心问题：如何评价保形预测方法在组成型输出上的可靠性？不同方法在哪些条件下更适用？**\n\n请你作为独立研究者：\n\n1. 梳理现有保形方法与评价方式的做法与局限，明确你要回答的具体研究问题与假设；\n2. 设计并实现能回答你的研究问题的实验方案，说明选择依据，并量化结果的不确定性；\n3. 可以使用自行生成的合成数据，也可以使用公开真实数据集（可用数据的类别见 `data.md`）；\n4. 如能给出理论分析，给出推导并用实验核对；\n5. 说明你的结论在什么条件下成立、哪些条件下不应外推。\n\n以研究问题和实验证据为主线，自行决定具体路线。\n\n**证据标准与关键约束**\n结论必须有可复现的实验证据支持，并报告不确定性；可以得出支持、反驳或视条件而定的结论，负面或与预期不符的结果同样可以接受。\n\n时间上限一周（含阅读、实现、实验、写作），以 CPU 为主、至多数个 GPU 小时，存储 < 10 GB，不需要付费 API（见 `resources.md`）；可用数据类别见 `data.md`；产出是一篇完整的研究论文及全部代码（见 `deliverable.md`）。\n\n**起步文献清单**\n以下文献是该领域较早的背景文献，仅作背景知识参考。取证日期：2026-10-27。\n\n1. Vovk, Gammerman, Shafer. *Algorithmic Learning in a Random World.* Springer, 2005。说明：保形预测的专著，介绍该框架的理论基础与算法。\n2. Papadopoulos, Proedrou, Vovk, Gammerman. *Inductive confidence machines for regression.* ECML 2002（无 arXiv 版）。说明：提出面向回归的归纳（inductive）置信机，将模型训练与置信度校准使用不同的数据。\n3. Lei, G'Sell, Rinaldo, Tibshirani, Wasserman. *Distribution-Free Predictive Inference For Regression.* arXiv:1604.04173（2016-04-14）。https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.04173 。说明：为回归提出基于保形推断的无分布假设预测区间框架，对 full 与 split 两类保形变体做了理论和实证比较。\n4. Barber, Candès, Ramdas, Tibshirani. *Predictive inference with the jackknife+.* arXiv:1905.02928（2019-05-08）。https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.02928 。说明：提出 jackknife+ 预测区间，利用留一预测构造区间，并给出覆盖率保证。\n5. Tibshirani, Barber, Candès, Ramdas. *Conformal Prediction Under Covariate Shift.* arXiv:1904.06019（2019-04-12）。https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06019 。说明：把保形预测推广到训练与测试协变量分布不同的情形，提出加权保形预测并给出覆盖率保证。\n6. Angelopoulos, Bates. *A Gentle Introduction to Conformal Prediction and Distribution-Free Uncertainty Quantification.* arXiv:2107.07511（2021-07-15）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07511 。说明：保形预测与无分布不确定性量化的入门综述，含示例。\n7. Angelopoulos, Bates, Malik, Jordan. *Uncertainty Sets for Image Classifiers using Conformal Prediction.* arXiv:2009.14193（2020-09-29）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.14193 。说明：为图像分类器构造预测集的保形算法，在大规模图像分类数据集上评估。\n8. Ding, Angelopoulos, Bates, Jordan, Tibshirani. *Class-Conditional Conformal Prediction with Many Classes.* arXiv:2306.09335（2023-06-15）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.09335 。说明：针对类别数很多、每类标注样本有限的分类问题，提出聚类保形预测（clustered conformal prediction）并在多个数据集上评估。\n9. Jung, Noarov, Ramalingam, Roth. *Batch Multivalid Conformal Prediction.* arXiv:2209.15145（2022-09-30）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.15145 。说明：提出批量设定下的多重有效（multivalid）保形预测算法，并给出理论保证与实验评估。\n10. Javanmardi, Stutz, Hüllermeier. *Conformalized Credal Set Predictors.* arXiv:2402.10723（2024-02-16）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.10723 。说明：提出用保形预测为分类任务预测 credal set（概率分布的集合）的方法。\n11. Cresswell, Kumar, Sui, Belbahri. *Conformal Prediction Sets Can Cause Disparate Impact.* arXiv:2410.01888（2024-10-02）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01888 。说明：通过人类被试实验研究保形预测集在人机协作决策中对不同受保护群体的影响。\n12. Aitchison. *The statistical analysis of compositional data.* JRSS-B 44(2), 1982（无 arXiv 版）。说明：组成数据（和为常数的向量）统计分析的经典文献。\n\n**数据与获取方式**\n取证日期：2026-10-27。可以使用下列公开数据、其他公开数据，或完全使用自行生成的合成数据。\n\n| 数据 | 获取 | 许可/账号 | 规模与格式 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| CIFAR-10 | https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html （重定向到 https://cave.cs.toronto.edu/kriz/cifar.html ，HTTP 200）；或 `torchvision.datasets.CIFAR10` | 页面未给出许可证，只要求使用时引用技术报告 Krizhevsky 2009（Learning Multiple Layers of Features from Tiny Images）；无需账号 | 60000 张 32x32 彩色图像、10 类，50000 训练 + 10000 测试；python 版压缩包 `cifar-10-python.tar.gz` 163 MB（170498071 字节，md5 `c58f30108f718f92721af3b95e74349a`）；文件自 2009-06-04 起未变 |\n| 合成数据 | 自行生成 | 无 | 生成方式和规模由你设计。无外部依赖，无许可问题。 |\n| 其他公开数据 | 可在 torchvision、scikit-learn、Hugging Face Datasets、OpenML 等常用数据集库检索 | 选用时请自行核实 | 输出为概率向量、比例或分布（和为 1 的非负向量）的公开数据集均可，包括分类数据集上分类器的输出概率。请在复现说明中记录所用数据的许可证和版本（需要账号或同意协议的数据，须说明获取方式）。 |\n\n**资源与预算**\n- 计算：以 CPU 为主即可（8 核以上、16 GB 内存为宜）。若需要自己训练预测器，可使用一张 GPU（至多数小时），也可使用现成的预训练模型或小型模型。\n- 估算依据（粗估，未实测）：数据量级 10^3–10^4 个样本；实验阶段单次运行通常为秒级以内；整个研究的实验阶段总算力预计在数十个 CPU 小时量级，另需至多数个 GPU 小时准备预测器。实际用量取决于你设计的实验规模。\n- 付费 API：不需要。\n- 时间上限：一周（含阅读、实现、实验、写作）。\n- 存储：< 10 GB。\n- 联网：可访问公开文献与数据下载；不得访问 deliverable.md 中列出的禁止内容。\n\n**产出要求**\n**提交物**\n1. 一篇完整的研究论文（PDF 与 LaTeX 源文件）。从 ICML、ICLR、NeurIPS 中自选一个会议，使用该会议\n   最新一届的官方 LaTeX 模板，遵守其正文页数上限（参考文献、附录是否计入页数按该会议的规定）；\n   该会议要求随文提交的 checklist（例如 NeurIPS 的 paper checklist）一并填写。论文至少包含摘要、\n   引言、相关工作、方法、实验、讨论与局限、参考文献。\n2. 全部代码与复现说明：环境与依赖版本、数据获取步骤和所用数据的版本、随机种子、运行命令和预计\n   耗时，以及论文中每张表、每张图由哪个脚本生成。\n\n**评审标准**\n论文按所选会议主会的审稿标准评审，重点看：\n\n- 新颖性与定位：贡献是什么，与已有工作的关系和区别是否讲清楚；\n- 严谨性：方法和实验设计是否合理，基线、对照和消融是否恰当，是否报告不确定性（多次运行、\n  置信区间或显著性检验）；\n- 证据是否充分：实验能否支撑论文的每个结论，结论的适用范围是否写清楚；\n- 表达：论文是否清楚、完整，他人能否据此复现；\n- 局限：是否如实讨论方法和结论的局限与失效情形。\n\n负面结果、与预期不符的结果同样有价值；结论有充分证据、不确定性报告完整，比结论本身是正面还是\n负面更重要。\n\n**如实报告（禁止编造）**\n- 论文中的每个数字、每张表和每张图都必须来自你在本任务中实际运行、随附代码可以重新产生的结果；\n  不得编造数据或结果，不得用占位数字、估计值或\"示意\"性的合成结果冒充实测结果。\n- 失败的运行、与预期不符的结果和放弃的方案如实报告；因预算没有完成的实验写明未完成，不写成\n  已完成。\n- 只引用真实存在、你确实查阅过的文献，不得编造引用。\n\n**不得访问的内容**\n本任务的研究问题已有一篇已发表的论文研究过。为保证研究独立，不得检索、访问、阅读或使用：\n\n- 那篇论文本身，及其任何镜像、预印本、摘要页、会议或评审页面、幻灯片、海报、视频、博客和代码\n  仓库；\n- 引用、讨论或复现那篇论文的任何页面，包括后续论文、综述、论文笔记、论文聚合站点和社交媒体\n  讨论。\n\n也不要尝试推测或检索那篇论文的标题、作者或结果。`starter_literature.md` 中的文献，以及与那篇\n论文无关的其他公开文献、数据和代码，可以正常使用。若在检索中偶然遇到上述内容，立即停止阅读，\n并在复现说明中如实记录遇到了什么、在哪里遇到、读到了多少。\n\n# Rubrics\n\nNo rubric is provided for this task; the requirements stated above apply.\n# Cross-fitted boundary-adaptive predictor: scope and reproducibility\n\nAttempt: `cycle02_cross_fitted_ilr_residual_geometry_attempt_01`\nIdea: `cycle02_cross_fitted_ilr_residual_geometry`\nCandidate: `program_1`\n\n## Available contract\n\nThe `program.py:handle` RPC contract is unchanged. Training supplies positive\ncompositions, five covariates, and an ILR basis. Predictions can return only an\nILR location and a lower-triangular positive shape factor. Calibration and all\nevaluation belong to the harness. No additional response keys are introduced.\n\nThe requested Hellinger/Euclidean/smoothed-log-ratio set comparison, groupwise\nconformal ranks, synthetic structural-zero experiments, paired test coverage\nintervals, and common-simplex volume probes cannot be expressed through this\ninterface. In particular, adjusting a shape is not groupwise calibration, and\nan ILR ellipsoid does not implement a Hellinger ball. No such mechanisms or\nassociated results are claimed. The input contract excludes structural zeros.\n\n## Implemented bounded change\n\nThe incumbent quadratic features and ridge model forms are retained. For at\nleast four training rows, this candidate replaces the incumbent's single 75/25\nlocation/residual split with four-fold out-of-fold ILR residuals. Row index\nmodulo four fixes fold membership deterministically. Each row's residual uses\na location ridge fitted on the other three folds, with penalty 0.01. The final\nlocation ridge is then refitted on all training rows with the same penalty.\nThe variance ridge uses all out-of-fold residuals, targets\n`log(oof_residuals ** 2 + 0.02)`, and penalty 5.0; it never uses final-model\nin-sample residuals. No query covariates or calibration responses enter fitting.\nAll constants below are unchanged and fixed beforehand rather than selected on\nevaluation performance.\n\n1. Invert fitted ILR locations to simplex compositions using stable softmax.\n   This is an inverse-coordinate prediction, not an oracle conditional mean.\n2. Freeze tertile edges of the minimum final-model predicted component on all\n   training rows.\n   Assign every row with right-sided search, including tied edges. Ties can\n   leave groups empty; no jitter or response-based reassignment is used.\n3. Standardize every out-of-fold training ILR residual by the fitted variance\n   scales, `exp(0.5 * clip(design @ variance, -6, 5))`. Clip standardized\n   residuals at magnitude 1000 for numerical safety.\n4. Estimate a pooled second-moment shape with eight identity pseudo-observations.\n   Shrink each group's second moment towards that pooled shape with 32\n   pseudo-observations. Empty groups use the pooled shape exactly. This models\n   residual cross-coordinate dependence without unstable unregularized inverses.\n5. At prediction, return `diag(scales) @ chol(group_shape)` for the frozen\n   predicted-boundary group. This is a fixed row-wise function, independent of\n   query ordering, query size, other queries, and previous prediction transfers.\n\nFor fewer than four training rows, fit ridge locations to all rows and use unit\nshapes, avoiding an empty location or residual fitting partition. Shrinkage\nmakes every shape positive definite, including constant-predictor and empty-group\ncases. Factor matrices remain full row-major lower-triangular matrices of the\ndocumented dimensions. Transport encoding and state transitions are unchanged.\n\n## Interpretation and limitations\n\nThis is only a training-fitted, boundary-adaptive baseline for the existing\nellipsoid interface. It may improve or worsen efficiency and local coverage.\nFold-model residuals may overstate full-model errors. Each row's response is\nexcluded from its own fold location fit, but variance and shape estimates reuse\ntraining information; residuals are not independent, and no unbiasedness or\nconditional-coverage theorem is claimed. The final location, variance, and\nshapes must be frozen before independent external calibration. Under\nexchangeability, the usual split-conformal marginal guarantee relies on that\nexternal calibration, not on cross-fitting itself.\nIt cannot provide training-fixed-group conformal coverage guarantees because\ncalibration is not performed separately within these groups. Even genuine\ngroup-calibrated coverage would concern covariate-defined groups, not events\ndefined by the newly observed response. Nothing here identifies latent class\nprobability coverage from categorical labels.\n\nThere are no executed experiments, confidence intervals, measured improvements,\npaper tables, or figures from this editing stage. Execution, external retrieval,\nand tests were forbidden. No prohibited literature was accessed. No PDF or\nconference-paper evidence is fabricated. The harness alone runs the official\nevaluation after submission. Its supplied seeds initialize the preserved RPC\nstate; the fitted predictor itself is deterministic conditional on training.\nThe manifest and entrypoint remain those of the starter; NumPy and Python's\nstandard library remain the only imported dependencies. Dependency versions\nand runtime have not been measured in this stage.\n# Continuous boundary covariance interpolation\n\nAttempt: `cycle03_continuous_boundary_covariance_attempt_01`\nIdea: `cycle03_continuous_boundary_covariance`\nCandidate: `program_1`\n\n## Contract and bounded change\n\nThe starter manifest, entrypoint `program.py:handle`, RPC response keys, transfer\nencoding, shapes, and operation/state transitions are unchanged. Only training\ncompositions, five covariates, and the supplied ILR basis enter fitting. Queries\nreturn only ILR locations and lower-triangular positive shape factors. The\nharness owns conformal calibration; no calibration data or diagnostics are added.\n\nThe starter's quadratic features, four deterministic location folds, held-out\nILR residuals, all-row final location refit, log-squared-residual variance ridge,\nclipping, and ridge penalties remain unchanged. Training boundary tertiles and\nright-sided group assignment also remain unchanged. Pooled second moments retain\neight identity pseudo-observations; each group's second moment retains 32 pooled\npseudo-observations. Empty groups therefore retain the pooled shape.\n\nThe only substantive change is query covariance routing:\n\n1. Store the three shrunk group covariance matrices, not their Cholesky factors.\n2. Freeze representative training boundary-score quantiles at 1/6, 1/2, and 5/6.\n   Boundary score is the minimum component of the inverse-ILR final location.\n3. Collapse exactly identical knots with `np.unique`. Arithmetic-average the\n   corresponding covariance matrices without changing the original group fits.\n4. Interpolate adjacent distinct covariance matrices with clipped linear weights.\n   Scores outside the anchor range receive the endpoint matrix. A single unique\n   knot broadcasts its covariance for every query.\n5. Compute Cholesky after interpolation and return\n   `diag(scales) @ chol(query_shape)` with the original variance scales.\n\nFor fewer than four training rows, preserve the all-row ridge location and zero\nvariance coefficients. Return a single training-median knot and identity matrix\nusing the same four-part internal model schema as the normal path.\n\n## Positive definiteness, continuity, and scope\n\nFor a nonzero vector v, the pooled quadratic form is\n`(||standardized @ v||^2 + 8 ||v||^2) / (n + 8) > 0`.\nEach group shape is a positive weighted combination of its positive-semidefinite\nsecond moment and that positive-definite pooled shape. Empty groups equal the\npooled shape. Duplicate-anchor arithmetic averages remain positive definite.\nFor any two positive-definite anchors A and B and weight t in [0,1],\n`v.T @ ((1-t) A + t B) @ v > 0`. Interpolation is therefore positive definite\nin exact arithmetic; Cholesky is taken on that interpolated covariance.\nThe minimum anchor eigenvalue bounds every interpolated minimum eigenvalue from\nbelow, and the maximum anchor eigenvalue bounds every maximum eigenvalue from\nabove. Positive diagonal variance scaling preserves positive definiteness.\nNo new stabilization constants, bandwidth, feature choices, or searches are used.\n\nThe clamped piecewise-linear covariance is continuous in boundary score: each\ninterval agrees with its neighbors at the shared anchor and is constant beyond\nthe extreme anchors. Single-anchor output is constant. Cholesky is continuous on\nthe positive-definite cone; this does not assert differentiability at knots.\nBoundary score and scale use each query independently and only frozen training\nparameters; neither batch statistics nor query fitting is performed. Singleton\nqueries retain a leading row axis through the broadcast/interpolation path.\n\nThese facts do not prove efficiency improvement, smaller volume, conditional\ncoverage, or an informative boundary statistic. Smooth residual dependence might\nfavor interpolation; abrupt dependence or an uninformative boundary statistic\nmight not. Cross-fitted residuals remain dependent and can overstate full-model\nerrors. Training variance and shape estimates reuse residual information. Only\nexternal split-conformal calibration can supply the usual marginal guarantee\nunder the required exchangeability and calibration assumptions, not a\nboundary-group guarantee. Positive-composition inputs exclude structural zeros.\nThe interface cannot implement alternative score families, separate group\ncalibration, or extra diagnostics; none is claimed.\n\n## Reproducibility and evidence status\n\nThe implementation is deterministic conditional on supplied training data.\nPython's standard library and NumPy remain the only imported dependencies;\ndependency versions and runtime were not measured at this editing stage.\nNo project code, tests, benchmarks, evaluation, network calls, or exploratory\ncomputation were executed. Constant-input, tied-anchor, small-training-set, and\nsingleton-query handling were reviewed statically only, not empirically verified.\nThe harness performs the official evaluation after submission.\n\nNo measurements are available from this candidate. Improvement, coverage,\nvolume, paired-seed uncertainty, and mechanism attribution are unassessed.\nAggregate-only measurements, if later released, cannot establish paired-seed\nuncertainty or isolate the scientific mechanism. No paper, PDF, tables, figures,\nor completed experiments are claimed. No prohibited literature was accessed.\nThis artifact is the bounded candidate implementation, not the full research\nsubmission described by the broader task.\n44:CASE_IDS = ('C1', 'C2', 'C3', 'C4', 'C5', 'C6')\n67:    def __init__(self, category):\n139:    def __init__(self, stream):\n155:    def _accept(self, chunk):\n181:    def _drain(self):\n486:    def __init__(self, arguments, environment, cwd=None):\n534:    def __init__(self, path, work_seconds, reset_seconds):\n585:            self.resets.append(request['seed'])\n818:    if manifest['plane'] != PLANE or manifest['seeds'] != SEEDS or manifest['counts'] != list(COUNTS):\n820:    if manifest['corpus_rng']['version'] != 2 or manifest['corpus_rng']['reset_seed_is_data_seed'] is not False:\n852:        datasets[(item['case'], item['seed'])] = (covariates, responses, entropy)\n853:    expected = {(case, seed) for case in CASE_IDS[:4] for seed in SEEDS}\n859:def quantile(scores, alpha=.1):\n860:    rank = math.ceil((len(scores) + 1) * (1 - alpha))\n925:    strata = []\n929:            strata.append(float(covered[mask].mean()) if mask.any() else 0.)\n930:    return {'coverage': coverage, 'volume': volume, 'strata': strata,\n968:              'ilr_basis': basis.tolist(), 'alpha': .1, 'nbytes': flat.nbytes, 'chunks': chunks}))\n1008:    case, seed = episode['case'], episode['seed']\n1009:    parent = 'C2' if case in ('C5', 'C6') else case\n1010:    all_x, all_y, _ = datasets[(parent, seed)]\n1016:    if case == 'C5':\n1024:    if case == 'C6':\n1039:def worst_case(episode):\n1040:    return {'case': episode['case'], 'seed': episode['seed'], 'complete': False, 'score': 0.,\n1041:            'coverage': 0., 'volume': 1., 'strata': [0.] * 4,\n1067:                          rpc_cap, clock, rpc, evaluate, worst_case, record_failure, cleanup):\n1070:    outcomes = [worst_case(episode) for episode in episodes]\n1086:            retained = worst_case(episode)\n1115:            require_reset_ack(rpc({'op': 'reset', 'seed': episode['seed']}, timeout=timeout))\n1173:    if episode['case'] == 'C5':\n1175:    if episode['case'] == 'C6':\n1200:    parent = 'C2' if episode['case'] in ('C5', 'C6') else episode['case']\n1201:    probe_rng = private_rng(datasets[(parent, episode['seed'])][2], 'volume_probes')\n1202:    probes = probe_rng.dirichlet(np.ones(dimension + 1), size=(anchors, 128 if smoke else PROBES))\n1227:                  volume=statistics['volume'], strata=statistics['strata'], gaps=gaps,\n1234:def fixed_weight_score(values, weights, worst_case):\n1239:    retained = [worst_case if value is None else value for value in values]\n1245:def aggregate(outcomes, seeds):\n1247:    expected_slots = len(CASE_IDS) * len(seeds)\n1256:        strata = np.array([value['strata'] for value in primary]).reshape(6, len(seeds), 4)\n1257:        result['worst_stratum_coverage'] = float(strata.mean(axis=1).min())\n1266:def evidence(outcomes, seeds):\n1269:    seed_values = np.array([[value['score'] for value in primary if value['seed'] == seed] for seed in seeds])\n1270:    mean_per_seed = seed_values.mean(axis=1)\n1272:    boot = rng.choice(mean_per_seed, size=(2000, len(seeds)), replace=True).mean(axis=1)\n1274:    comparison_values = np.array([[value['gaps'] for value in primary if value['seed'] == seed]\n1275:                                  for seed in seeds]).mean(axis=1)\n1276:    resample = rng.integers(0, len(seeds), size=(2000, len(seeds)))\n1278:    for seed in seeds:\n1279:        selected = [value for value in primary if value['seed'] == seed]\n1280:        all_episodes = [value for value in outcomes if value['seed'] == seed]\n1281:        cases = []\n1283:            cases.append({key: item for key, item in value.items()\n1285:        records.append({'seed': seed, 'score': float(np.mean([value['score'] for value in selected])),\n1288:                        'solution': {'cases': cases, 'seed_bootstrap_score95': score_interval,\n1289:                                     'paired_seed_bootstrap_comparisons95': comparison_interval,\n1290:                                     'uncertainty_policy': 'paired seed resampling across all six cases; few-seed intervals unstable; four sign strata descriptive, no simultaneous conditional-coverage claim',\n1291:                                     'replay_completed': bool(outcomes[-1]['complete']) if seed == seeds[0] else None}})\n1304:             'alpha': .1, 'nbytes': training.nbytes, 'chunks': math.ceil(training.nbytes / CHUNK)}\n1350:    statistical_seeds = SEEDS[:1] if smoke else SEEDS\n1351:    episodes = [{'case': case, 'seed': seed} for case in CASE_IDS for seed in statistical_seeds]\n1352:    episodes.append({'case': 'C1', 'seed': statistical_seeds[0]})\n1369:    outcomes = [worst_case(episode) for episode in episodes]\n1381:            worst_case=worst_case, record_failure=record_failure, cleanup=client.close)\n1382:        if client.resets != [episode['seed'] for episode in episodes]:\n1392:            outcomes[0] = worst_case(episodes[0])\n1412:        metrics = aggregate(outcomes, statistical_seeds)\n1420:            observations = evidence(outcomes, statistical_seeds)\n1422:                'planned': [episode['seed'] for episode in episodes], 'actual': client.resets,\n1423:                'complete': client.resets == [episode['seed'] for episode in episodes]}\n\"\"\"Training-only boundary-adaptive ILR ellipsoid predictor, finite JSON RPC v1.\n\nAll operations return EXACTLY the keys described here; extra keys are forbidden.\nNo paths, metrics, test targets, calibration responses, or plane identity are inputs.\nSeeds initialize randomness only; accept any nonnegative signed-64-bit integer seed.\nreset {op,seed} -> {ok:true}: discard model and both in-memory transfer buffers.\n\nfit_start {op,n_rows,composition_dim,ilr_basis,alpha,nbytes,chunks} -> {ok:true}:\none training phase per reset; 1<=n_rows<=4096, K=composition_dim in {3,8},\nalpha=0.1, ilr_basis is K by K-1 orthonormal contrast matrix. Training consists\nof row-major float64 X[n_rows,5], then Y[n_rows,K] (positive, row sums one).\nnbytes=8*n_rows*(5+K), chunks=ceil(nbytes/1048576), at most four chunks.\nfit_chunk {op,index,block} -> {ok:true}: consecutive zero-based indices only.\nfit_commit {op} -> {ok:true}: require all bytes; fit actual predictor, free upload.\nAll training inputs are authorized; calibration is performed by the evaluator.\n\npredict_start {op,x:block} -> {n_rows,dimension,nbytes,chunks,encoding,dtype,shape}:\nrequires committed fit and no live prediction. X has shape [N,5], 1<=N<=5952.\nReturn dimension d=K-1; buffer is row-major float64 [N,d+d*d], location first,\nthen a FULL row-major lower triangular Cholesky factor of a positive shape.\nshape=[N,d+d*d], encoding='base64', dtype='<f8', nbytes=8*N*(d+d*d),\nchunks=ceil(nbytes/1048576)<=4. A prediction must be a fixed row-wise function\nof X after fit: no test-time updating, batch position, or batch-size dependence.\npredict_chunk {op,index} -> {block}: ordered chunks of flattened output.\npredict_release {op} -> {ok:true}: requires all chunks read; erase output buffer.\nThe evaluator performs three prediction transfers: full authorized query batch,\nreordered query subset, and another subset, without revealing partition boundaries.\n\nEvery block has EXACT keys {encoding,dtype,shape,nbytes,data}: base64 ASCII data,\nencoding='base64', dtype='<f8', shape a positive integer list, nbytes=8*prod(shape).\nChunk blocks use shape [number_of_float64_values], at most 1048576 raw bytes.\nAll numeric values must be finite. Location and factor magnitudes <=1e6;\nfactor diagonals >=1e-8; singular-value condition number <=1e10.\nAt most 4 MiB per logical buffer, 512 MiB total candidate-owned memory allowed;\ncomplete framed UTF-8 JSON messages <8 MiB (and always <64 MiB). No disk exchange.\nNo optional response keys or candidate diagnostics are supported. Errors are protocol\nfailures, not a channel for scientific self-reports. Reset is the only restart.\nEvaluator controls include matched constant inputs, genuine training derangement,\nactual point-copy singleton/full-simplex membership, query invariance, and replay.\nNeither nominal coverage nor superiority is an eligibility gate: negative results\nare valid when execution and required instrumentation are valid. Search utility is\n1 - .8*max(0,.9-coverage)/.9 - .2*normalized_volume, equally weighted case then seed.\n\"\"\"\nimport base64\nimport math\nimport numpy as np\n\nCHUNK = 1048576\nSTATE = {}\n\n\ndef decode(block):\n    if set(block) != {'encoding', 'dtype', 'shape', 'nbytes', 'data'}:\n        raise ValueError('invalid block')\n    if block['encoding'] != 'base64' or block['dtype'] != '<f8':\n        raise ValueError('invalid encoding')\n    shape = block['shape']\n    if not isinstance(shape, list) or not shape or any(type(size) is not int or size < 1 for size in shape):\n        raise ValueError('invalid shape')\n    size = math.prod(shape) * 8\n    if size != block['nbytes'] or size > 4 * CHUNK:\n        raise ValueError('invalid size')\n    raw = base64.b64decode(block['data'], validate=True)\n    if len(raw) != size:\n        raise ValueError('invalid byte count')\n    array = np.frombuffer(raw, dtype='<f8').reshape(shape)\n    if not np.isfinite(array).all():\n        raise ValueError('nonfinite')\n    return array\n\n\ndef encode(array):\n    array = np.asarray(array, dtype='<f8', order='C')\n    return {'encoding': 'base64', 'dtype': '<f8', 'shape': list(array.shape),\n            'nbytes': array.nbytes, 'data': base64.b64encode(array.tobytes()).decode('ascii')}\n\n\ndef features(covariates):\n    columns = [np.ones(len(covariates))]\n    columns.extend(covariates[:, index] for index in range(5))\n    columns.extend(covariates[:, index] ** 2 for index in range(5))\n    columns.extend(covariates[:, first] * covariates[:, second]\n                   for first in range(5) for second in range(first))\n    return np.column_stack(columns)\n\n\ndef ridge(design, targets, penalty):\n    return np.linalg.solve(design.T @ design + penalty * np.eye(design.shape[1]), design.T @ targets)\n\n\ndef predicted_boundary(means, basis):\n    logits = means @ basis.T\n    weights = np.exp(logits - logits.max(axis=-1, keepdims=True))\n    predictions = weights / weights.sum(axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n    return predictions.min(axis=-1)\n\n\ndef boundary_groups(predicted_boundary_score, boundary_edges):\n    boundary_group = np.searchsorted(boundary_edges, predicted_boundary_score, side='right')\n    return boundary_group\n\n\ndef fit_model(covariates, responses, basis):\n    transformed = np.log(responses) @ basis\n    design = features(covariates)\n    dimension = basis.shape[1]\n    if len(covariates) < 4:\n        location = ridge(design, transformed, 0.01)\n        variance = np.zeros((design.shape[1], dimension))\n        train_boundary_scores = predicted_boundary(design @ location, basis)\n        boundary_edges = np.quantile(train_boundary_scores, [1.0 / 3.0, 2.0 / 3.0])\n        group_factors = np.repeat(np.eye(dimension)[None, :, :], 3, axis=0)\n        return location, variance, boundary_edges, group_factors\n    fold_count = 4\n    fold_ids = np.arange(len(covariates)) % fold_count\n    oof_residuals = np.empty_like(transformed)\n    for fold_index in range(fold_count):\n        held_out = fold_ids == fold_index\n        fold_location = ridge(design[~held_out], transformed[~held_out], 0.01)\n        oof_residuals[held_out] = transformed[held_out] - design[held_out] @ fold_location\n    final_location = ridge(design, transformed, 0.01)\n    variance = ridge(design, np.log(oof_residuals ** 2 + 0.02), 5.0)\n    train_boundary_scores = predicted_boundary(design @ final_location, basis)\n    boundary_edges = np.quantile(train_boundary_scores, [1.0 / 3.0, 2.0 / 3.0])\n    residual_groups = boundary_groups(train_boundary_scores, boundary_edges)\n    scales = np.exp(0.5 * np.clip(design @ variance, -6, 5))\n    standardized = np.clip(oof_residuals / scales, -1000.0, 1000.0)\n    identity = np.eye(dimension)\n    pooled_shape = (standardized.T @ standardized + 8.0 * identity) / (len(oof_residuals) + 8.0)\n    group_factors = np.empty((3, dimension, dimension))\n    for group_index in range(3):\n        group_residuals = standardized[residual_groups == group_index]\n        group_shape = (group_residuals.T @ group_residuals + 32.0 * pooled_shape) / (len(group_residuals) + 32.0)\n        group_factors[group_index] = np.linalg.cholesky(group_shape)\n    return final_location, variance, boundary_edges, group_factors\n\n\ndef handle(request):\n    operation = request['op']\n    if operation == 'reset':\n        if set(request) != {'op', 'seed'} or type(request['seed']) is not int or not 0 <= request['seed'] < 2**63:\n            raise ValueError('invalid reset')\n        STATE.clear()\n        STATE['rng'] = np.random.default_rng(request['seed'])\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if 'rng' not in STATE:\n        raise ValueError('reset required')\n    if operation == 'fit_start':\n        if 'upload' in STATE or 'model' in STATE:\n            raise ValueError('one fit per reset')\n        rows, size = request['n_rows'], request['composition_dim']\n        if type(rows) is not int or not 1 <= rows <= 4096 or size not in (3, 8):\n            raise ValueError('invalid training shape')\n        raw_size = rows * (5 + size) * 8\n        if request['nbytes'] != raw_size or request['chunks'] != math.ceil(raw_size / CHUNK):\n            raise ValueError('invalid transfer')\n        basis = np.asarray(request['ilr_basis'], dtype=float)\n        if basis.shape != (size, size - 1) or not np.allclose(basis.T @ basis, np.eye(size - 1)):\n            raise ValueError('invalid basis')\n        STATE.update(upload=bytearray(), metadata=dict(request), basis=basis, upload_index=0)\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if operation == 'fit_chunk':\n        if 'upload' not in STATE or request['index'] != STATE['upload_index']:\n            raise ValueError('invalid upload order')\n        block = decode(request['block'])\n        expected = min(CHUNK, STATE['metadata']['nbytes'] - len(STATE['upload']))\n        if block.ndim != 1 or block.nbytes != expected or expected < 1:\n            raise ValueError('invalid upload length')\n        STATE['upload'].extend(block.tobytes())\n        STATE['upload_index'] += 1\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if operation == 'fit_commit':\n        metadata = STATE['metadata']\n        if len(STATE['upload']) != metadata['nbytes'] or STATE['upload_index'] != metadata['chunks']:\n            raise ValueError('incomplete upload')\n        rows, size = metadata['n_rows'], metadata['composition_dim']\n        flat = np.frombuffer(STATE['upload'], dtype='<f8')\n        covariates = flat[:rows * 5].reshape(rows, 5)\n        responses = flat[rows * 5:].reshape(rows, size)\n        STATE['model'] = fit_model(covariates, responses, STATE['basis'])\n        STATE['dimension'] = size - 1\n        del flat, covariates, responses\n        del STATE['upload']\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if operation == 'predict_start':\n        if 'prediction' in STATE or 'model' not in STATE:\n            raise ValueError('invalid prediction state')\n        covariates = decode(request['x'])\n        if covariates.ndim != 2 or covariates.shape[1] != 5 or not 1 <= len(covariates) <= 5952:\n            raise ValueError('invalid query shape')\n        design = features(covariates)\n        location, variance, boundary_edges, group_factors = STATE['model']\n        dimension = STATE['dimension']\n        means = design @ location\n        scales = np.exp(0.5 * np.clip(design @ variance, -6, 5))\n        groups = boundary_groups(predicted_boundary(means, STATE['basis']), boundary_edges)\n        factors = scales[:, :, None] * group_factors[groups]\n        output = np.concatenate((means, factors.reshape(len(covariates), -1)), axis=1).astype('<f8')\n        STATE['prediction'] = output.tobytes()\n        STATE['prediction_index'] = 0\n        return {'n_rows': len(covariates), 'dimension': dimension, 'nbytes': output.nbytes,\n                'chunks': math.ceil(output.nbytes / CHUNK), 'encoding': 'base64',\n                'dtype': '<f8', 'shape': list(output.shape)}\n    if operation == 'predict_chunk':\n        if request['index'] != STATE['prediction_index']:\n            raise ValueError('invalid download order')\n        start = request['index'] * CHUNK\n        raw = STATE['prediction'][start:start + CHUNK]\n        if not raw:\n            raise ValueError('invalid download index')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
